'''Fosian Baliscans''' ({{lang-bl|Baliscanos Fosianos}}) are [[Baliscan people|Baliscans]] of [[Fosian people|Fosian]] ancestry. The term refers to a [[W:Panethnicity|pan-ethnic]] group that includes diverse populations, which have origins in [[Central Fosia|Central]], [[East Fosia|East]], [[North Fosia|North]] and [[Northeast Fosia|Northeast]], [[West Fosia|West]], or [[South Fosia]], as defined by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS). This includes numerous reported entries such as [[Paran|Parani]], [[Zong]], [[Indian Baliscans|Kemali]] ([[W:Dravidian peoples|Dravidian]], [[W:Rajasthani people|Rajasthani]], [[W:Gujarati people|Gujarati]], etc.) [[Nusarayan Baliscans|Nusarayan]], [[Jade Baliscans|Jade]], [[Dagiti Baliscans|Dagiti]], [[Sanese Baliscans|Sanese]], [[Yanbarun Baliscans|Yanbarun]], [[Galastani Baliscans|Galastani]], [[Fenian Baliscans|Fenian]], [[Kwangju Baliscans|Kwangju]] and [[Pansundanese Baliscans|Pansundanese]], amongst others. 

Although migrants from Fosia have been in parts of the contemporary Baliscan Confederation since the 17th century (including [[Ryuji Takemoto]] and [[Cala del Drac]] during the [[Great Disappointment]]), large-scale immigration of Fosians did not begin until the mid-19th century. Unlike other [[W:Settler colonialism|settler colonial]] states such as the [[Markland]], Balisca did not enact racial and nativist immigration laws prohibiting non-Illypnian migration, and subsequently went on to accept significant numbers of Fosian immigrants, specifically from [[Bayan]], [[Zong]], [[Greater Kemal]], [[Northeast Fosia]] ([[Dagit]] and [[Nusaraya]]), [[Kwangju]], the [[Sanese archipelago]] ([[Okaiken]], [[Nakamizu]] and [[Yanbaru]]) and [[Balausia]].

Government immigration policy was significantly expanded following independence, offering generous guarantees of land and pensions to anyone willing to migrate to the archipelago. Analyses of the [[2015 Baliscan census|2015]] and [[2020 Baliscan census|2020]] censuses have shown that Fosian Baliscans are the second-fastest growing ethnic group in Balisca, behind [[Kafto-Baliscans]].

